o help Landlord.\nReason: To ensure the Landlord expends a high card or to let the Teammate lead if the Landlord cannot beat J. This saves my high cards and helps the team.\nAnswer: {"cards": []}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord (Next Player) has only 2 cards left, making this a critical phase. My Teammate (Prev Player) has played a Jack (J) over the Landlord\'s 5. This is a strong move intended to either win the trick or force a high card from the Landlord. As the player acting immediately before the Landlord (in the sequence Teammate -> Me -> Landlord), I act as the "gatekeeper". Beating my teammate\'s J with a K, A, or 2 would be counterproductive: it would waste my high cards, remove control from my teammate (who has a better hand count of 9 vs my 17), and potentially free the Landlord from spending a high card.\n\nBy passing, I force the Landlord to make a decision. If the Landlord cannot beat the J, my teammate wins the trick and continues to lead, which is ideal. If the Landlord plays a high card (like Q, K, A, or 2) to beat the J, they will be down to one card. I still hold the Black Joker and a 2, allowing me to catch the Landlord\'s high card on the next opportunity (immediately after they play, assuming standard play or via the next round) or ensure they deplete their strength. Passing is the most strategic option to support the team.\n\nReason: My teammate is controlling the table. Interfering now would disrupt the team\'s tempo. I must save my high cards (2, Black Joker) to stop the Landlord if they play a high card to beat the Jack.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
